Can the script properly split it?

A naive script (like simple regex on headers) will fail, but a two-pass hierarchical parser with context inheritance can do it reliably.

The challenge with exam papers is that question structures are fundamentally hierarchical, not flat.

The Three Traps of Naive Splitting
The Shared Stem / Context Loss Trap:
Question 2 often starts with a 3-paragraph experiment description, an apparatus diagram, and a data table. Then come sub-parts (a), (b)(i), and (c). If a script naively splits at every (a) or (b), sub-part (b) will become completely incomprehensible because it loses the initial diagram and experiment setup.

Mark Scheme vs. Question Paper Format Divergence:
Question papers are written in descriptive prose and lists, whereas Edexcel mark schemes are rendered by GLM-OCR as markdown tables with columns (Question number, Answer, Mark, Additional guidance). Splitting logic for a question paper will break on a mark scheme.

Multi-Part Marks in Single Table Rows:
In mark schemes, examiners sometimes group sub-parts together into one row (e.g., Question 1(a)(i) and (ii)), or put multiple marks (M1,A1,B1) on separate lines inside a single table cell.

How to Build a Parser That Actually Works
To split both QP.md and MS.md cleanly into atomic JSON entities, your script in syllabai-parser needs a Tree-Based (Hierarchical) Parser:

Question (e.g. Question 1)
 │
 ├── Stem Context (Intro text, apparatus diagram, parent tables)
 │
 ├── Sub-Part: (a)
 │    ├── Own prompt text
 │    ├── Inherited Stem Context
 │    └── Mark Allocation (e.g. 2 marks)
 │
 └── Sub-Part: (b)
      ├── Sub-Sub-Part: (i)
      └── Sub-Sub-Part: (ii)
Step 1: Parse the Question Paper (QP.md)
Root Splitting: Split on primary question boundaries using regex:

Python
# Matches "1", "Question 1", "# Question 1", or "1 (a)"
r'(?:^|\n)(?:#+\s*)?(?:Question\s*)?(\d+)\s*(?:\([a-z]\)|\n)'
Stem Extraction: The text, equations, and images between the question header and the first sub-part (a) are flagged as parent_stem_markdown.

Sub-part Splitting: Look for standard Edexcel lettering patterns:

Level 1: (a), (b), (c)

Level 2: (i), (ii), (iii)

Context Inheritance: When generating the JSON for (b)(i), assemble the prompt as:

Rendered Markdown=Parent Stem+Sub-part Stem+Specific Question Text
(This ensures that when rendered on the frontend in "Target Test" or "Exam Mode", the student still sees the diagram).

Step 2: Parse the Mark Scheme (MS.md)
Because GLM-OCR outputs Edexcel mark schemes as pipe tables, parse the markdown tables row by row:

Extract rows where the first column matches the question number pattern (e.g., 1(a), 1(b)(i)).

Extract the Mark column value (convert strings like "2" or "[2]" into integers).

Extract Answer and Additional guidance columns into the mark_scheme JSON object.

Automatic Validation Guardrails (CI Checks)
To ensure the script split everything correctly without human intervention on every file, add these automated sanity checks:

Mark Sum Check: Sum the extracted marks of all parts in Question 1. Does it match (Total for Question 1 = X marks) stated at the bottom of the question? If it does not, throw an error flag.

Paper Total Check: Sum all extracted questions. For Edexcel Chemistry 4CH1, does Paper 1C total exactly 110 marks, and Paper 2C total exactly 70 marks? If not, the script missed a sub-part.

1:1 Mapping Check: Verify that every question_id generated from QP.md has an exact matching entry in the parsed MS.md dictionary.

Recommended Implementation Approach
Write this as a modular Python utility in syllabai-parser (e.g., scripts/atomize_paper.py) that accepts:

Bash
python scripts/atomize_paper.py \
  --qp Past-Papers/paper\ 1/2024-06/QP.md \
  --ms Past-Papers/paper\ 1/2024-06/MS.md \
  --out corpus/atomized/4CH1_2024_06_1C.json
This ensures full reproducibility: if you ever improve the extraction logic or re-run OCR, running the script regenerates all your atomic question parts in seconds.

